      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nThis is a French (LNE) OIML Certificate of Conformity for water meters (R49/2013), certificate number R 49/2013-FR2-15.01 rev2.\n\nKey data:\n- Certificate number: R 49/2013-FR2-15.01 rev2\n- Scheme: B (OIML-CS certificate, marked \"OIML-B 04-9-2013\" vertical text)\n- Issuing authority: Laboratoire National de Métrologie et d'Essais (LNE)\n- Person responsible: Thomas LOMMATZSCH, Responsable du Pôle Certification (Measuring Instruments Division Manager)\n- Applicant: ITRON FRANCE, 11 boulevard Pasteur, 67500 HAGUENAU, FRANCE\n- Manufacturer: ITRON FRANCE, 11 boulevard Pasteur, 67500 HAGUENAU, FRANCE\n- Certified type: water meter ITRON type WOLTEX (WE), also MWE mentioned in tables\n- Date issued: October 18th, 2016 (2016-10-18)\n- Member state: FRANCE\n- Page total: 4 pages\n- Recommendation: OIML R49/2013\n- Evaluation report: LNE n°P163010 (29 pages)\n- LNE reference: LNE-23936 rév. n° 4\n\nModels: The tables cover WE50/MWE50, WE65/MWE65, WE80/MWE80, WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125, WE150/MWE150, WE200/MWE200, WE250/MWE250, WE300/MWE300.\n\nLet me define the models:\n- WE50/MWE50\n- WE65/MWE65\n- WE80/MWE80\n- WE100/MWE100, WE125/MWE125 (combined in one column for some rows)\n- WE150/MWE150\n- WE200/MWE200\n- WE250/MWE250\n- WE300/MWE300\n\nNote the pattern: WE = mechanical, MWE = ? Probably the MWE is a variant (maybe with pulse output or similar). The type designation is WOLTEX (WE) — the tables show both WE and MWE variants share the same characteristics.\n\nNow let me parse the characteristics tables.\n\n**Table 1 (Page 1 of annex):**\n\n| Type | WE50/MWE50 | WE65/MWE65 | WE80/MWE80 | WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125 | WE150/MWE150 |\n\nWait — the first table has 8 columns after \"Type\": looking at the header: WE50/MWE50, WE65/MWE65, WE80/MWE80, colspan=2 \"WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125\", colspan=2 \"WE150/MWE150\". So the data columns: for Q3 row: 40, 63, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400 — that's 7 values. Hmm.\n\nWait, the header has columns: Type, WE50/MWE50, WE65/MWE65, WE80/MWE80, WE100-WE125 (colspan 2), WE150 (colspan 2). That's 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 8 columns total. Data rows:\n\n- DN: 50, 65, 80, \"100/125\" (colspan 2), \"150\" (colspan 2) — 1+1+1+1+1+1 = 6 cells but with colspans covering 8 columns.\n- Longueur: \"200/210/300/312\", \"200/220/225/300\", \"200/220/225/350\", \"250/290\" (colspan 2), \"300/340\" (colspan 2)\n- Raccordements: Brides DN 50, DN 65, DN 80, \"Brides DN 100 et DN125\" (colspan 2), \"Brides DN 150\" (colspan 2)\n- Totalisateurs: TVM / Verre Métal (colspan 7)\n- Portée du totalisateur (m³): \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\" (colspan 5), \"9 999 999*\" (colspan 2)\n- Échelon de vérification (dm³): \"0.2 ou 2\" (colspan 5), \"2*\" (colspan 2)\n- Volume cyclique théorique (dm³): \"20\" (colspan 5), \"200\" (colspan 2)\n- Débit permanent Q3 (m3/h): 40, 63, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400 — 7 individual cells\n- Débit de surcharge Q4 (m³/h): 50, 78.7, 78.7, 125, 200, 312.5, 500 — 7 cells\n- Dynamique Q3/Q1: 63, 63, 63, 100, 160, 100, 160 — 7 cells\n- Dynamique Q2/Q1: 1.6 (colspan 7)\n\nSo the Q3/Q4/Dynamique rows have 7 columns, while the header has effectively 5 logical groups. This means the WE100-WE125 group spans 2 columns (100 and 125), and WE150 spans 2 columns... wait but then 7 columns: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, then WE150 spanning 2? Hmm.\n\nLet's think: 7 data columns. Logical types: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150. That's 6. With 7 columns, one group must span 2. The header colspan=2 for WE100-WE125 and colspan=2 for WE150. So the 7 columns might be: WE50(1), WE65(1), WE80(1), WE100-125(2 columns: 100, 125), WE150(2 columns?). Hmm, 1+1+1+2+2 = 7. Yes! 7 columns.\n\nSo Q3 values: 40 (WE50), 63 (WE65), 63 (WE80), 100 (WE100), 160 (WE125), 250 (WE150?), 400 (WE150?).\n\nHmm wait, but WE150 has only one DN = 150. Why 2 columns? Let me check Q4: 50, 78.7, 78.7, 125, 200, 312.5, 500. If WE150 = 400 m³/h Q3, then Q4 for 400 would be 500. Q3=250 → Q4=312.5. Hmm.\n\nActually maybe there's a hidden column: perhaps WE150 has two variants? Let me reconsider. Actually wait — maybe the columns are: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150, WE150 (different version?). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the last two columns correspond to DN150 with Q3=250 and DN150 with Q3=400? That doesn't seem right for water meters.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: For DN150, Itron Woltex: Q3 = 250 m³/h with Q3/Q1 = 100? and Q3 = 400 with 160? Actually the Dynamique Q3/Q1 row: 63, 63, 63, 100, 160, 100, 160.\n\nHmm interesting. If WE150 has Q3=250 with ratio 100... wait but for a given Q3, Q3/Q1 lower values are permitted. Let me think about what's plausible:\n\nActually wait. Let me recount the colspan structure. Header: `<td>Type</td><td>WE50/<br>MWE50</td><td>WE65/<br>MWE65</td><td>WE80/MWE80</td><td colspan=\"2\">WE100-WE125/ MWE100-<br>MWE125</td><td colspan=\"2\">WE150/ MWE150</td>`. So data grid = 7 columns: col1=WE50, col2=WE65, col3=WE80, col4-5=WE100-WE125, col6-7=WE150.\n\nQ3 row: `<td>40</td><td>63</td><td>63</td><td>100</td><td>160</td><td>250</td><td>400</td>` — 7 cells: col4=100 (WE100), col5=160 (WE125), col6=250 (WE150), col7=400 (WE150)?? That gives WE150 two Q3 values: 250 and 400.\n\nDynamique Q3/Q1: 63, 63, 63, 100, 160, 100, 160: WE100=100, WE125=160, WE150=100 or 160?? Hmm.\n\nThat's weird. But wait — maybe it's the reverse: WE150 has DN 150 and the two columns might be two different variants. Hmm, but the header says WE150 spans both.\n\nAlternative reading: maybe the original PDF table had columns: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150 (6 columns), and one of the header cells spans to align. The Q3 row has 7 entries though: 40, 63, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400.\n\nHmm, actually maybe Q3: WE50=40, WE65=63, WE80=63?? No wait, WE80 should be 63? Hmm, typical: DN80 → Q3=63 m³/h. DN100 → Q3=100... wait but then WE125 → Q3=160? DN125 → 250? and DN150 → 400?\n\nLet's check Q4: 50, 78.7, 78.7, 125, 200, 312.5, 500. Q4 = 1.25×Q3: 40→50 ✓, 63→78.75≈78.7 ✓, 63→78.7 ✓, 100→125 ✓, 160→200 ✓, 250→312.5 ✓, 400→500 ✓. Good, consistent.\n\nNow for the mapping: if col4-5 = WE100-WE125 with Q3 = 100 (WE100) and 160 (WE125), then col6-7 = WE150 with Q3=250 and 400?? That means DN150 has two variants: Q3=250 and Q3=400? Possible for Woltex: the Woltex WE150 might exist in Q3=250 (ratio 100?) hmm...\n\nWait, look at Dynamique Q3/Q1: 63, 63, 63, 100, 160, 100, 160. If col6 (WE150, Q3=250) has ratio 100 and col7 (WE150, Q3=400) has 160... Hmm.\n\nAlternatively maybe the mapping is: col4=WE100 (Q3=100, dyn 100), col5=WE125 (Q3=160, dyn 160), col6=WE150 (Q3=250, dyn 100), col7=WE150 (Q3=400, dyn 160)? But the note says \"For a given Q3, values of Q3/Q1 lower than those in the table are permitted\" — meaning higher ratios shown but lower allowed. Hmm, that suggests these are maximum ratio values offered.\n\nHmm, but wait. Actually maybe I'm misreading and it's actually 6 logical columns where \"WE100-WE125\" spans 2 columns and \"WE150\" spans... Let me recount the header cells with colspan: Type(1), WE50(1), WE65(1), WE80(1), WE100-125(2), WE150(2) = 8 grid columns total! Type + 7 data columns.\n\nData rows:\n- DN row: Type, \"50\", \"65\", \"80\", \"100/125\" (colspan2), \"150\" (colspan2) = 1+1+1+1+2+2 = 8 ✓\n- Q3 row: Type + 7 plain cells = 8 ✓\n\nSo grid = 8 columns, data columns = 7: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150a, WE150b.\n\nHmm, so WE150 genuinely has 2 sub-columns. But DN says 150 for both. Length: 300/340 for both (colspan 2). Connections: Brides DN 150 (colspan 2). Totaliser range: 9 999 999* (colspan 2). Verification interval: 2* (colspan 2). Cyclic volume: 200 (colspan 2).\n\nSo WE150 exists in two variants: Q3=250 (dyn 100) and Q3=400 (dyn 160)? Hmm, but wait — actually could it be that the second-to-last column is actually DN125 with a different... no, header clearly says WE150 for cols 6-7.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe the intended mapping is:\n- col4 = WE100 (Q3=100)\n- col5 = WE125 (Q3=160)\n- col6 = WE150 (Q3=250, dyn Q3/Q1=100)\n- col7 = WE150 variant (Q3=400, dyn 160)?\n\nActually, thinking about real Itron Woltex meters: Woltex WE DN150 has Q3 = 250 m³/h or 400 m³/h options? Actually, Itron Woltex 150mm: Q3=250 with R=100? Hmm, the Woltex WE DN150: Q3 = 250 m³/h (Q3/Q1=100) hmm not sure. But it doesn't matter — I should extract what's in the document.\n\nActually wait, maybe another reading: The \"Dynamique Q3/Q1\" for the WE150 columns are 100 and 160, meaning DN150 is available with Q3/Q1 = 100 (with Q3=250) or 160 (with Q3=400). Hmm, but the note says for a given Q3, lower ratios are permitted but not below 40.\n\nHmm, alternatively maybe the columns are: col4-5 = WE100-WE125 where col4=WE100 (Q3=100, dyn=100), col5=WE125 (Q3=160, dyn=160)... and col6-7 = WE150: col6 = Q3=250 dyn=100, col7 = Q3=400 dyn=160. Wait, but actually maybe it's: WE150 Q3=250 has dyn 100... hmm hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider once more. Another possibility: the second \"160\" under \"Dynamique Q3/Q1\" and the \"160\" Q3... Maybe the columns 6,7 represent WE150 with Q3=250 m³/h and DN... no.\n\nOK here's another thought — maybe there were originally 6 data columns and the OCR expanded a column. But we must work with what we have: 7 data columns with header groups WE100-WE125 (2 cols) and WE150 (2 cols).\n\nGiven the note \"Pour un débit nominal donné (Q3) des valeurs de Q3/Q1 inférieures à celles figurant dans le tableau ci-dessus sont permises\" — for DN50-80: ratio 63; DN100: 100; DN125: 160; DN150: 100 or 160 depending on Q3.\n\nHmm wait, actually that doesn't quite work either since for DN125 with Q3=160 ratio 160, lower permitted but not below 40.\n\nLet me just be faithful: I'll create models:\n- WE50/MWE50: DN50, Q3=40, Q4=50, dyn Q3/Q1=63\n- WE65/MWE65: DN65, Q3=63, Q4=78.7, dyn=63\n- WE80/MWE80: DN80, Q3=63, Q4=78.7, dyn=63\n- WE100/MWE100: DN100, Q3=100, Q4=125, dyn=100\n- WE125/MWE125: DN125, Q3=160, Q4=200, dyn=160\n- WE150/MWE150 (Q3=250): DN150, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, dyn=100\n- WE150/MWE150 (Q3=400): DN150, Q3=400, Q4=500, dyn=160\n\nThen WE200/MWE200: DN200, length 350, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, dyn=40\nWE250/MWE250: DN250, length 450, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, dyn=40\nWE300/MWE300: DN300, length 500, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, dyn=80\n\nHmm, but wait. For the WE100-WE125 mapping, is it possible that col4=WE100 with Q3=100 and col5=WE125 with Q3=160? Yes that's most natural.\n\nAlternatively the mapping might be col4 (WE100) = Q3 100, col5 (WE125) = Q3 160. Yes.\n\nNow hold on, maybe I should reconsider whether col6/col7 might be \"WE150\" and \"WE150\" but actually one might be... Hmm, given header colspan=2 for WE150 and there are exactly 7 values, and the DN row uses colspan=2 for \"150\", the two columns are both DN150. So WE150 has two flow variants: Q3=250 (R100) and Q3=400 (R160). Actually wait, hmm, but could the two DN150 columns be distinguished by Q3/Q1 ratio 100 vs 160 for the SAME Q3? No — Q3 differs (250 vs 400).\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider: what if col6 = Q3=250 belongs to WE125 and col7 = 400 to WE150? No — col4-5 are WE100-WE125 which is exactly 2 columns for 2 sizes. So col6-7 both WE150. OK.\n\nHmm, but actually, real Itron Woltex WE DN150: Q3 options 250 and 400 m³/h. Yes! Itron Woltex WE 150: Q3 = 250 m³/h (R100 hmm) or 400 m³/h (R160?). Hmm... Actually per mid certificates, Woltex WE DN150 Q3=250 m³/h with R=100... I'm not 100% sure but the extraction is faithful regardless.\n\nNow, page 2 table (for WE50-WE150):\n\n- Orientation: Horizontale ou Verticale (all)\n- Classe d'exactitude: 2 (accuracy class 2)\n- Classe d'environnement: B/O (environmental class B, ... \"B/O\" — probably climatic class B and others O? In R49, environmental classes: B = outdoor? Actually R49 defines classes B, O, X... Class B = outdoor installations, O = other? I'll record as string \"B/O\".)\n- Pression Maximale Admissible (bar): 20 → mapd = 20 bar\n- Classe de sensibilité au profil d'écoulement: WE50/MWE50: U0SD0; WE65, WE80: U3SD0 (colspan 2 covering WE65 and WE80); WE100-WE125 & WE150: U0D0 (colspan 2... wait).\n\nLet me look: `<td>U0SD0</td><td colspan=\"2\">U3SD0</td><td colspan=\"2\">U0D0</td>`. Grid is 7 columns: col1=U0SD0 (WE50), col2-3=U3SD0 (WE65, WE80), col4-7=U0D0 (WE100, WE125, WE150a, WE150b). \n\nHmm wait, but page 2 table header: `<td>Type</td><td>WE50/<br>MWE50</td><td>WE65/<br>MWE65</td><td>WE80/MWE80</td><td>WE100-WE125/ MWE100-<br>MWE125</td><td>WE150 / MWE150</td></tr>` — this header has only 5 data columns (no colspans!). But the sensitivity row has U0SD0 + colspan2 U3SD0 + colspan2 U0D0 = 5 cells → 1+2+2 = 5 grid columns. OK so on page 2, the grid is 5 columns: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100-125, WE150.\n\nSo sensitivity: WE50=U0SD0, WE65=U3SD0, WE80=U3SD0, WE100-125=U0D0, WE150=U0D0.\n\n- Perte de pression à Q3 (bar): `&lt;16 | colspan2 &lt;40 | &lt;10 (DN100)&lt;16 (DN125) | &lt;25 (DN100)&lt;40 (DN125) | &lt;16`. Wait, that's 4 cells for 5 columns? Let me count: `<td>&lt;16</td><td colspan=\"2\">&lt;40</td><td>&lt;10 (DN100)<br>&lt;16 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;25 (DN100)<br>&lt;40 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;16</td>` = 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6 grid columns?? That exceeds 5.\n\nHmm wait: cells: td(<16), td colspan=2(<40), td(<10 DN100 / <16 DN125), td(<25 DN100 / <40 DN125), td(<16). Total grid = 1+2+1+1+1 = 6. But header grid = 5. Inconsistent OCR/parsing, but whatever — the meaning: WE50: <0.16? Hmm wait, \"<16\"? Pressure loss <16 bar?? That can't be right — pressure loss at Q3 of 16 bar is absurd. It's probably <0.16 bar, <0.40 bar, <0.10, <0.16, <0.25, <0.40, <0.16. The OCR dropped \"0.\"! Typical water meter pressure loss classes: Δp10, Δp16, Δp25, Δp40, Δp63 (in units of 0.1 bar i.e. classes per ISO 4064 / EN 14154: pressure loss classes Δp10 = 0.1 bar, Δp16 = 0.16 bar, Δp25=0.25, Δp40=0.40, Δp63=0.63).\n\nSo \"Perte de pression à Q3 (bar)\": WE50: <16?? Hmm. Actually wait — maybe the unit is not bar but something else. The row label says \"(bar)\". Values <16, <40, <10, <16, <25, <40, <16. In units of... kPa? <16 kPa = 0.16 bar! <40 kPa = 0.4 bar. <10 kPa = 0.1 bar. <25 kPa = 0.25 bar. So these are probably kPa values mislabeled, or the label should be interpreted as \"bar × 100\"? Hmm. Actually EN 14154 pressure loss classes: Δp10, Δp16, Δp25, Δp40, Δp63 where the number = pressure loss in units of 10 kPa? No wait: Δp class 40 means maximum pressure loss of 0.4 bar (40 kPa). Yes! So <16 → class Δp16 → 0.16 bar = 16 kPa.\n\nAnd page 4 table for WE200-300 says \"Classe de perte de pression (selon EN 14154 à Q3): Δp25\" — consistent: DN200-300 class Δp25 = 25 kPa = 0.25 bar.\n\nSo the page 2 values <16, <40, <10, <16, <25, <40, <16 — with \"(bar)\" — are likely \"0.16 bar\" etc. with OCR dropping \"0.\". But I shouldn't fabricate. The safest approach: record the value as \"<16\" with unit as written? Hmm. The rules say don't fabricate. I could record pressure_loss_at_q3 with value {max: 16} and unit \"kPa\"? No — the label says bar.\n\nHmm. Tricky. Options:\n1. Record as value {max: 16}, unit \"bar\" — faithful to OCR but physically wrong.\n2. Record as string \"<16 (DN100)...\" preserving original.\n3. Interpret as class labels.\n\nGiven the mapping per column: col1 (WE50): <16; col2-3 (WE65-80): <40; col4 (WE100-125): \"<10 (DN100)\\n<16 (DN125)\"; col5 (WE150): \"<25 (DN100)\\n<40 (DN125)\"; col6 (WE150): <16.\n\nWait, the last cell \"<16\" — with grid 6 columns vs header 5... The assignment: WE50: <16; WE65+WE80: <40; WE100-WE125: <10 (DN100), <16 (DN125); WE150: <25 (DN100), <40 (DN125)... and extra <16?\n\nHmm confusing. Let me re-read the raw: `<td>Perte de<br>pression à Q3<br>(bar)</td><td>&lt;16</td><td colspan=\"2\">&lt;40</td><td>&lt;10 (DN100)<br>&lt;16 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;25 (DN100)<br>&lt;40 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;16</td>`\n\nCells: [<16], [<40, span2], [<10 DN100/<16 DN125], [<25 DN100/<40 DN125], [<16].\n\nGrid positions (7-col grid of page 1? or 5-col grid of page 2?). Page 2 grid from its header = 5 columns: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100-125, WE150.\n\nIf this row is on the 5-col grid: 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 5... but there are 5 data cells → 1+2+1+1+1 = 6 > 5. One extra. Maybe one of the cells shouldn't be there or spans differently. Possibly the original table row was: WE50: <16; WE65-80: <40; WE100-125: <10 (DN100)/<16 (DN125); WE150: <25 (DN100)/<40 (DN125)... and the trailing <16 might belong elsewhere (maybe an artifact or another column for WE150 variant 400?).\n\nHmm, if grid is 7 columns (like page 1): WE50: <16; WE65: <40; WE80: <40 (span2 covers col2-3); WE100: <10; WE125: <16 (the combined cell spans col4 only?? no...).\n\nI think the most sensible interpretation on the 7-column grid (consistent with page 1): col1=WE50 <16; col2-3=WE65-80 <40; col4-5=WE100-125: <10 (DN100)/<16 (DN125); col6-7=WE150: <25 (DN100)/<40 (DN125)... then trailing <16 has no column. Hmm, unless col7 (WE150 Q3=400 variant): <16.\n\nActually hmm — maybe: col6 (WE150 DN100?? no, WE150 is DN150)...\n\nWait wait. \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" — but WE100-WE125 are DN100 and DN125! And the next cell says \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" again?? That can't be for WE150 (DN150). Something's off.\n\nLet me re-read: `<td>&lt;10 (DN100)<br>&lt;16 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;25 (DN100)<br>&lt;40 (DN125)</td>`. Hmm, both mention DN100/DN125. That's weird for a WE150 column. Unless the original table had different layout: maybe columns were DN100 and DN125 separately with pressure losses, i.e., the 7-column grid is: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150, WE150, and the pressure loss row: <16, <40, <40, <10(DN100)... \n\nHold on, let me re-map on 7-col grid: cells: [WE50:<16], [WE65+WE80:<40 (span 2)], [col4: <10 (DN100)], [col5: <16 (DN125)], [col6-7?? : <25 (DN100)/<40 (DN125)], [<16 extra]. Hmm no.\n\nAlternatively on 7-col: [<16]=WE50, [<40 span2]=WE65,WE80, [<10 DN100, <16 DN125] spans... if this cell spans col4 only, then [<25 DN100, <40 DN125] spans col5, and [<16] spans col6-7? So: WE100 (DN100): <10; WE125 (DN125): <16; WE150 (both variants): <25?? Hmm but the cell says \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" which mentions DN100/DN125 — contradicts WE150=DN150.\n\nUnless... the \"(DN100)\" annotations are actually \"(Δp100)\"? or \"(Δ DN100)\"? Hmm. Or the cell is \"<25 (Δp class) ...\" \n\nYou know what — this is likely OCR mangling. The physically sensible reading (based on EN14154 pressure-loss classes and page 4's Δp25):\n\n- WE50 (DN50): Δp class... <0.16 bar (Δp16)\n- WE65/80: <0.40 bar (Δp40)\n- WE100 (DN100): <0.10 bar (Δp10)\n- WE125 (DN125): <0.16 bar (Δp16)\n- WE150: <0.25 bar (DN100??) hmm...\n\nWait, maybe the parenthetical is \"(Δp100)\"? no...\n\nAlternatively the annotation might be different: maybe it says \"<10 (DN100) / <16 (DN125)\" for WE100-WE125 column meaning: DN100: <10 kPa, DN125: <16 kPa. And for WE150 column: \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" might actually be \"<25 (DN150)/...\" OCR error? Hmm, or maybe WE150 also came in DN100/DN125?? No.\n\nHmm, actually! Maybe the second-to-last cell annotation refers to *meters tested on DN100 and DN125 test benches*? Unlikely.\n\nLet me try yet another interpretation: perhaps the columns of the pressure-loss row don't align with the type columns at all, and the table in the original PDF is:\n\n| | WE50/MWE50 | WE65/MWE65 | WE80/MWE80 | WE100-WE125 | WE150 |\n| Perte de pression à Q3 (bar) | <0.16 | <0.40 | <0.10 (DN100), <0.16 (DN125) | <0.25 (DN100), <0.40 (DN125) | <0.16 |\n\nWait that gives 5 cells: <0.16 (WE50), <0.40 (WE65-80), <10/<16 (WE100-125), <25/<40 (WE150???)... no, WE100-125 gets two cells.\n\nUgh. OK. Honest approach: This is genuinely ambiguous OCR. I'll record the pressure-loss row in `matrix_tables` preserving the raw cell text, and in model_level characteristics I could record pressure_loss_at_q3 as string values preserving the raw annotation, or skip it in model_level and keep it in matrix_tables.\n\nActually, the rules say don't fabricate; preserving raw strings in matrix_tables rows is safest for ambiguous data. For model_level, I can include the unambiguous ones... hmm, but even \"<16 bar\" is suspicious.\n\nDecision: I'll preserve the table as matrix_tables entry \"Pressure loss at Q3 (page 2)\" with raw cells, and in model_level record `pressure_loss_at_q3` with string values exactly as shown (e.g., \"<16\", \"<40\", \"<10 (DN100) / <16 (DN125)\", \"<25 (DN100) / <40 (DN125)\") — but as strings with unit \"bar\" as labeled. Hmm, but rule 2 says values must be structured objects, numbers not strings... but formulas/symbols can be strings. A \"<16\" comparison could be {min: null, max: 16}. Given the OCR may have dropped \"0.\", {max: 16, unit: \"bar\"} is risky but faithful to the document as OCR'd.\n\nAlternative: treat them as class designations: Δp16, Δp40, etc. Page 4 uses \"Δp25\" explicitly, which supports that page 2's \"<16\" means class Δp16 → 16 kPa = 0.16 bar. But converting 16 → 0.16 would be fabrication-ish.\n\nI think the best is to keep faithful: value {max: 16}, unit \"bar\", and note in description? Hmm. Actually, you know, let me reconsider. The document literally says \"(bar)\" and \"<16\". If I output {max: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, that's faithful to the text. A downstream consumer can interpret. But it's physically absurd (16 bar pressure loss in a water meter at Q3 while MAP is 20 bar).\n\nHmm, let me think about what's most useful. The schema wants faithful extraction. I'll be faithful: value {max: 16} etc., unit \"bar\", and add the DN annotations into the condition or keep as footnote-ish. Actually for the combined cells I'll use config_level-ish? No — simpler: use matrix_tables with raw text and ALSO include model_level entries with string values like \"<16\" hmm...\n\nRule 2: \"Pure units or footnote markers are NOT values\" — \"<16\" is a threshold, fine as {max: 16}.\n\nI'll do: model_level pressure_loss_at_q3 with values:\n- WE50/MWE50: {max: 16}\n- WE65/MWE65: {max: 40}\n- WE80/MWE80: {max: 40}\n- WE100/MWE100: {max: 10} (DN100)\n- WE125/MWE125: {max: 16} (DN125)\n- WE150 (Q3=250): {max: 25}? and (Q3=400): {max: 40}? Hmm — the cell says \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" which mentions DN100/DN125. If it's for WE150, mentioning DN100/125 is wrong...\n\nHmm hold on, let me recount the columns. Maybe I misread the page-2 pressure row structure. Raw: `<td>&lt;16</td><td colspan=\"2\">&lt;40</td><td>&lt;10 (DN100)<br>&lt;16 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;25 (DN100)<br>&lt;40 (DN125)</td><td>&lt;16</td>`\n\nHmm wait — maybe the page-2 table grid is actually 6 columns because the header row I quoted: `<td>Type</td><td>WE50/MWE50</td><td>WE65/MWE65</td><td>WE80/MWE80</td><td>WE100-WE125/ MWE100-MWE125</td><td>WE150 / MWE150</td>` = 5 data columns. But sensitivity row: U0SD0, U3SD0(span2), U0D0(span2) = 5 data grid cols. Pressure row: 6 data grid cols. One of these rows is misaligned by OCR.\n\nIf pressure row is right (6 cols): maybe the header should have been: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150 — 6 columns! And sensitivity: U0SD0(WE50), U3SD0(WE65,WE80), U0D0(WE100,WE125,WE150... span 2 covers only 2 though).\n\nArgh. OK here's my best coherent reconstruction given page 1 clearly has 7 data columns (WE100 and WE125 separate; WE150 with 2 variants... wait, page 1's WE150 spans 2 columns).\n\nHmm, page 1 grid: 7 data columns: [WE50][WE65][WE80][WE100][WE125][WE150?][WE150?].\n\nPage 2 sensitivity row maps as: [WE50: U0SD0][WE65,WE80: U3SD0][WE100,WE125,WE150a,WE150b: U0D0]. OK.\n\nPage 2 pressure row (6 cells → maybe spans onto the 7-col grid): [WE50: <16][WE65,WE80: <40][WE100: <10 (DN100)][WE125: <16 (DN125)][WE150a,WE150b: <25...?]. Hmm, that leaves [<25 (DN100)<40 (DN125)] and [<16] for two columns: WE150a: <25 (DN100)/<40 (DN125)?? WE150b: <16.\n\nHonestly, the \"(DN100)/(DN125)\" annotations look like they belong to the WE100-WE125 group. So maybe the actual PDF row was:\n\n| <0.16 | <0.40 | <0.10 (DN100) / <0.16 (DN125) | <0.25 (DN150)?? | <0.16 |\n\nHmm, wait — what if it's: WE100-WE125 column: \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\"; WE150 column: \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" where actually the parentheticals for WE150 should be about... no.\n\nNew idea: maybe the annotations are correct and the WE150 column contains two sub-columns DN100 and DN125?! Like \"WE150\" is a typo/OCR for two columns... no.\n\nOK, final decision: I will faithfully record the page-2 pressure-loss data in `matrix_tables` with raw strings, and in `model_level` I'll record `pressure_loss_at_q3` with the raw comparison strings as structured values ({max: N}, unit \"bar\") mapped to my best-effort model assignment, preserving the DN annotations in a `condition` field. Actually the structured value objects in model_level have shape {model, value, footnote_markers} — I can't add condition per value. Hmm, but rule 5 says config values follow {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\n\nAlternative simpler approach: put the pressure loss row ONLY in matrix_tables with raw text, and add model_level pressure_loss entries only where unambiguous:\n\n- WE50/MWE50: <16 (bar)\n- WE65/MWE65: <40\n- WE80/MWE80: <40\n- WE100/MWE100: <10\n- WE125/MWE125: <16\n- WE150/MWE150: <16 (last cell)\n\nand treat \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" as ambiguous → hmm, but then which model gets <25/<40?\n\nYou know, thinking about it more: on the 7-column grid, cells: col1=<16, col2-3=<40, col4=<10(DN100), col5=<16(DN125)... wait the cell \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\" — if the grid row is: [<16][<40 span2][<10DN100, <16DN125 — this might be TWO logical cells merged by OCR: <10 (DN100) for col4=WE100... no wait DN100→WE100? WE100 is DN100 yes! and <16 (DN125) for col5=WE125 (DN125) ✓. Then [<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)] — hmm, for col6-7 = WE150... DN100/DN125 again?? \n\nUnless the parentheticals are OCR duplicates. Or unless the last columns are actually: col6 = \"WE150\" with pressure loss <25 hmm.\n\nWait, actually — hold on. What if the page-2 table header lost a column? What if the real header is: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100-WE125, WE150, and the pressure row's 5 data cells are: WE50: <16; WE65+80: <40; WE100-125: <10 (DN100)/<16 (DN125); WE150: <25 (DN100)/<40 (DN125)?? — no, WE150's DN is 150.\n\nOR the parentheticals might be \"(Δp100)\"... or \"(Δp10 0)\" hmm.\n\nOR maybe they're \"(DN100)\" meaning \"for DN100 version\". Could WE150 fit DN100 flanges? No.\n\nHonestly — I bet the original PDF (Itron Woltex OIML cert) has:\n\nPerte de pression à Q3: WE50: <0,16 bar; WE65-80: <0,40 bar; WE100: <0,10 bar... hmm no wait.\n\nLet me look at actual data from similar Itron Woltex certs. The Woltex WE pressure loss: DN50: Δp63? I don't remember. DN100 Woltex: pressure loss at Q3 = 0.1 bar? DN125: 0.16? DN150: 0.25/0.4? Hmm, actually maybe: WE150 with Q3=250: Δp25? and with Q3=400: Δp40? And the \"(DN100)/(DN125)\" annotations might be OCR garbage from adjacent cells.\n\nGiven the last cell <16: maybe WE150 variant... ugh.\n\nFINAL DECISION: Put the full raw row in matrix_tables (page-2 table) with raw cell strings, and in model_level record pressure_loss_at_q3 for the clean assignments (WE50: 16, WE65: 40, WE80: 40, WE100: 10, WE125: 16) with a footnote-like condition, and for WE150 record both \"<25 (DN100) / <40 (DN125)\" raw and \"<16\"... no, that's messy.\n\nHmm, let me simplify. Actually — maybe I'm overanalyzing a colspan artifact. Let me count header cells again for page 2 table:\n\n`<tr><td>Type</td><td>WE50/<br>MWE50</td><td>WE65/<br>MWE65</td><td>WE80/MWE80</td><td>WE100-WE125/ MWE100-<br>MWE125</td><td>WE150 / MWE150</td></tr>` → 5 data columns.\n\nSensitivity: `U0SD0 | colspan2 U3SD0 | colspan2 U0D0` → 1+2+2 = 5 ✓. So: WE50: U0SD0; WE65-80: U3SD0; WE100-125 & WE150: U0D0. \n\nPressure: `<16 | colspan2 <40 | <10DN100,<16DN125 | <25DN100,<40DN125 | <16` → 1+2+1+1+1 = 6 ✗ (6≠5).\n\nIf pressure row had 5 cells without the trailing <16: 1+2+1+1 = 5 ✓: WE50: <16; WE65-80: <40; WE100-125: <10(DN100)/<16(DN125); WE150: <25(DN100)/<40(DN125)... but DN100/DN125 annotations on WE150 still wrong.\n\nOR if the 4th and 5th cells: WE100-125: <10 (DN100)/<16 (DN125) and WE150: <25.../<40.../ and trailing <16...\n\nWAIT. New idea! What if the annotations are \"(Δp100)\" → hmm no. What if they're \"(Δp10)\" and \"(Δp16)\" hmm. What if the original text is \"<10 (Δp10) <16 (Δp16)\"? and \"<25 (Δp25) <40 (Δp40)\"? That makes sense! Like: WE100-125: max 0.10 bar (class Δp10) / 0.16 bar (Δp16); WE150: 0.25 (Δp25) / 0.40 (Δp40); and then trailing <16?? still extra.\n\nHmm, or maybe the columns are: WE100: <10; WE125: <16; WE150: <25; WE150-400: <40; and... no.\n\nOK here's my true final decision, prioritizing fidelity over reconstruction:\n\nIn `matrix_tables`, I'll create entries reproducing the annex tables with raw cell strings, describing colspan ambiguity. In `characteristics.model_level`, I'll include attributes with clean numeric extraction: dn, length (as lists), q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, q2_q1_ratio, totalizer_range, verification_interval, cyclic_volume, map, accuracy_class, temperature_class, water_temperature_range, climatic_environment, orientation, flow_profile_sensitivity, reverse_flow. For pressure loss I'll include it with raw string values preserving annotations.\n\nActually hmm, for length: values like \"200/210/300/312\" — these are alternatives (the meter comes in multiple face-to-face lengths). I'll record as string \"200/210/300/312\" or as list? The schema's value can be a string. I'll keep as string with the raw alternatives, unit mm. Or maybe list of numbers? The value spec allows number | {min,max} | string. A slash list isn't a range; keep string.\n\nNow the temperature: Page 2: water temp class T30; climatique +5°C…+55°C. Page 4 (WE200-300): water temp +0.1°C … +30°C; environnement climatique +5°C…+55°C.\n\nNote: page 1-2 tables: WE50-WE150. Page 3-4: WE200, WE250, WE300. Note different characteristics between groups:\n- WE50-150: orientation horizontal OR vertical; WE200-300: horizontal only.\n- WE50-150: water temp class T30; WE200-300: water temp range +0.1 to +30°C (T30 class too but explicit range).\n- WE50-150: flow profile: U0SD0/U3SD0/U0D0; WE200-300: U0D0 (per EN14154).\n- WE50-150: pressure loss <16/<40/... bar; WE200-300: class Δp25.\n- WE50-150: MAP 20 bar; WE200-300: MAP 20 bar.\n- Totalizer: WE50-150: 999999 or 9999999 (WE150: 9999999*); WE200-300: 9999999 or 99999999.\n- Verification interval (dm³): WE50-150: 0.2 or 2 (WE150: 2*); WE200-300: 2 or 20.\n- Cyclic volume: WE50-80(?) & 100-125: 20 dm³; WE150: 200; WE200-300: 200.\n\nFootnote *: \"La version de totalisateur avec la portée de 99 999 999 m³ (échelon de vérification 20 dm³) est utilisable jusqu'à Q1= 3,125 m³/h inclus.\" — Hmm wait: portée 99 999 999 m³?? The table says \"9 999 999*\" for WE150. The footnote mentions 99 999 999 m³ range version with verification interval 20 dm³ usable up to Q1=3.125 m³/h. OK.\n\nFootnote **: \"Le compteur n'est pas conçu pour mesurer des flux inversés mais résiste à un flux inversé accidentel sans subir aucune détérioration ou modification des propriétés métrologiques.\"\n\nAlso notes: \"Pour un débit nominal donné (Q3) des valeurs de Q3/Q1 inférieures à celles figurant dans le tableau ci-dessus sont permises. Toutefois les valeurs de ce ratio ne peuvent être inférieures à 40.\" (page 1) — for WE50-150, min ratio 40. Page 3: \"...ne peuvent être inférieures à 10.\" — for WE200-300, min ratio 10.\n\nNow models list. Let me define model IDs:\n- WE50/MWE50\n- WE65/MWE65\n- WE80/MWE80\n- WE100/MWE100\n- WE125/MWE125\n- WE150 (Q3=250)/MWE150 (Q3=250)\n- WE150 (Q3=400)/MWE150 (Q3=400)\n- WE200/MWE200\n- WE250/MWE250\n- WE300/MWE300\n\nHmm, the WE150 two variants: I'll name them \"WE150/MWE150 (Q3=250 m³/h)\" and \"WE150/MWE150 (Q3=400 m³/h)\".\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me re-examine whether the 2 WE150 columns might instead be \"WE150\" and something else. Page 1 header: colspan=2 \"WE150/ MWE150\". Both DN 150. Length 300/340 for both. So two WE150 variants differing in Q3 (250 vs 400) and ratio (100 vs 160). This matches real Woltex: DN150 available with Q3=250 m³/h or 400 m³/h. \n\nAnd actually that also resolves pressure loss? WE150 (Q3=250): Δp25; WE150 (Q3=400): Δp40? That makes sense physically! Higher Q3 → higher pressure loss. And the trailing <16... hmm, doesn't fit.\n\nAnd what about \"(DN100) (DN125)\" annotations... For WE100-WE125 cell: \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\" — DN100=WE100 ✓, DN125=WE125 ✓. So the cell combined two sub-values. Then \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" for WE150... annotations should be DN150. OCR error, OR the original had \"(Δp25)\"... I'll just record the raw text in matrix_tables and make best-effort assignments in model_level with a note? The schema doesn't have notes per value. \n\nApproach: In model_level, for WE150 variants I'll record pressure_loss_at_q3 as raw strings: \"25 (DN100)...\" — no. Hmm.\n\nLet me just do this: model_level pressure_loss_at_q3 (unit bar, values as max):\n- WE50: 16\n- WE65: 40\n- WE80: 40\n- WE100: 10\n- WE125: 16\n- WE150 Q3=250: 25\n- WE150 Q3=400: 40\n\nAnd add a footnote capturing the raw annotation issue? Fabrication risk: assigning 25 to Q3=250 variant and 40 to Q3=400 variant is my interpretation. The raw cell order suggests: [<25(DN100)/<40(DN125)] then [<16]. If the last <16 is the 7th column (WE150 Q3=400), then: WE150a=<25(DN100)/<40(DN125), WE150b=<16. Hmm!\n\n7-col grid assignment: col1: <16 (WE50); col2-3: <40 (WE65, WE80); col4-5: <10 (DN100), <16 (DN125) → WE100, WE125 — wait but that cell is ONE cell \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\"... if each mention is one column: col4=<10 (DN100), col5=<16 (DN125) ✓. Then col6: \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" — mentions TWO... if two mentions→two columns but only col6-7 remain: col6=<25(DN100)?? col7=<40(DN125)?? But col6-7 are WE150 (DN150)! Then trailing <16 = 8th column?? There is no 8th.\n\nAlternatively grid: col1=<16, col2-3=<40, col4=\"<10(DN100)<16(DN125)\" (spans col4-5?? no, it's a single td...).\n\nYou know what, the OCR table structure is just broken here. Given the physical consistency argument (Δp classes 10/16/25/40 kPa and page 4's Δp25), and the pattern of increasing pressure loss with size: DN100: 0.1, DN125: 0.16, DN150: 0.25 (Q3=250) / 0.4 (Q3=400) — wait, or DN150: 0.25 & the Q3=400 variant 0.4? Hmm, and trailing <16?? Maybe trailing <16 is a duplicated artifact.\n\nHmm hold on, actually — maybe the last cell \"<16\" IS the WE150 value and \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" cell belongs to... ugh.\n\nLet me try assuming the true PDF table (before OCR mangling) for pressure loss is:\n\nWE50: <0.16 | WE65: <0.40 | WE80: <0.40 | WE100: <0.10 | WE125: <0.16 | WE150(Q3=250): <0.25 | WE150(Q3=400): <0.40\n\nwith annotations dropped/merged. Then OCR: <16, <40(span2), <10(DN100), <16(DN125), <25(DN100→?DN150?), <40, <16?? — doesn't yield the trailing <16.\n\nAlternatively true table:\nWE50: <16 | WE65+80: <40 | WE100-125: <10 (DN100), <16 (DN125) | WE150: <25 (both) | and <16... \n\nI give up on perfect reconstruction. DECISION: \n\n- matrix_tables: preserve both annex tables with raw strings exactly as OCR'd (I'll do one combined representation per page? The schema has matrix_tables[] with name/columns/rows/footnotes. I'll create matrix table entries for page 1 table (WE50-150), page 2 table (WE50-150 characteristics), page 3 table (WE200-300), page 4 table (WE200-300 characteristics). With raw row values preserving strings like \"<16\", \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\".)\n\n- model_level: pressure_loss_at_q3 with values as extracted best-effort: WE50: {max:16}, WE65: {max:40}, WE80: {max:40}, WE100: {max:10}, WE125: {max:16}, WE150 Q3=250: {max:25}, WE150 Q3=400: {max:40}, unit bar. Plus footnote marker referencing the ambiguity? No — I'll add a footnote? Footnotes are from the doc. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe safer: keep pressure loss ONLY in matrix_tables raw form, and skip in model_level to avoid wrong assignments. But rule 4 wants model_level populated... it says extract varying attributes; pressure loss does vary per model. The mis-assignment risk vs completeness... \n\nI'll include it in model_level with the values as OCR'd (16, 40, 40, 10, 16, 25, 40) and unit \"bar\", and include the DN annotations as footnote-ish condition via matrix_tables raw. I'll accept the interpretation; it follows the column order.\n\nHmm, wait, actually let me reconsider the assignment once more. The 7 values in order: 16, 40, 40, 10, 16, 25, 40 with annotations: [\"<16\"], [\"<40\"(×2)], [\"<10 (DN100)\", \"<16 (DN125)\"], [\"<25 (DN100)\", \"<40 (DN125)\"], [\"<16\"].\n\nColumn order: WE50, WE65, WE80, WE100, WE125, WE150a, WE150b.\nValue order: 16, 40, 40, 10, 16, 25, 40.\n\nDirect mapping: WE50:16, WE65:40, WE80:40, WE100:10, WE125:16, WE150a:25, WE150b:40. And annotations \"DN100/DN125\" on cells 4-5 (WE100 ✓ DN100, WE125 ✓ DN125) and on cell 6-7 (WE150a,b: DN100/DN125 ✗ should be DN150). The last \"<16\" has no home.\n\nSo the direct order mapping gives: WE150a: <25, WE150b: <40, and an orphan <16. OR the orphan <16 is WE150b and \"<25/<40\" cell spans... \n\nGiven the trailing \"<16\" comes after \"<25...<40\", and if cells were [c1]=<16, [c2-3]=<40, [c4]=<10(DN100), [c5]=<16(DN125), [c6]=<25(?), [c7]=<40(?)... then trailing <16 = c8 nonexistent. OR [c4-5]=\"<10(DN100)<16(DN125)\" as one merged cell covering WE100-125, [c6]=\"<25(DN100)<40(DN125)\"?? covering...\n\nIf [c6-7] = one merged cell \"<25 <40\" for WE150a,b and trailing <16 is... nothing. \n\nAlternatively the original might have had 8 columns with the last being \"MWE150\" separate?? Like WE150 | MWE150: <16?? Interesting!! If col6-7 were WE150 and col8 MWE150... no, header says WE150/MWE150 colspan 2.\n\nPhysical plausibility: For Woltex WE DN150 Q3=250: pressure loss class Δp25 (0.25 bar). For Q3=400: Δp40 (0.4 bar). This is exactly what Itron's datasheets show I believe. The trailing <16 might be an OCR duplication of the first cell.\n\nGoing with: WE150(Q3=250): 25; WE150(Q3=400): 40. And I'll note the raw in matrix_tables.\n\nHmm, one more consideration — simpler and safer: make the pressure loss row config_level? No. Final: model_level with the assignment above; matrix_tables preserve raw.\n\nNow other attributes:\n\nType-level (all models):\n- accuracy_class: 2 (integer) — \"Classe d'exactitude: 2\". R49 accuracy classes are 1 and 2. Value: 2, unit null.\n- environmental_class: \"B/O\" (page 2 only for WE50-150). For WE200-300 not listed. So model_group specific... hmm. WE50-150 have B/O; WE200-300 table doesn't mention it. I'll put it as type_level? It only applies to WE50-150. Could put in model_level with values for WE50-150 only, or as a group attribute. I'll include in model_level with values for the 7 small models... that's tedious but fine. Actually, maybe cleaner: characteristics.type_level holds whole-cert attributes; group-specific ones go to model_level. Environmental class B/O: assign to each WE50-150 model.\n\nHmm, but honestly \"B/O\" might mean classes B and O — R49-2013 defines environmental classes for water meters... Actually in R49, \"environmental class\" B = outdoor, O = other? There's class B (outdoor) and class O (indoor/other)? I'll keep raw \"B/O\".\n\n- max_admissible_pressure: 20 bar (all).\n- climatic_environment: +5…+55 °C (all).\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"N/A\" (all). Keep as string \"N/A\".\n- magnetic_environment_class: \"N/A\".\n- reverse_flow_measurement: \"No\" (all) with footnote **.\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6 (all).\n- totalizer type: \"TVM / Verre Métal\" (metal glass totalizer) — string. All.\n- orientation: WE50-150: \"Horizontal or Vertical\"; WE200-300: \"Horizontal\". → model_level.\n- water_temperature_class: T30 (WE50-150); WE200-300: water temperature range +0.1…+30 °C (T30 implied). I'll record water_temperature_class \"T30\" for WE50-150 and for WE200-300 record water_temperature_range {min:0.1, max:30} °C. Actually page 4 says \"Etendue de température de l'eau: +0.1°C … +30°C\" — that's the range; class T30 equivalent. I'll do model_level water_temperature_range for WE200-300 and water_temperature_class for WE50-150.\n\nModel-level numeric matrix:\n\nWE50/MWE50: DN 50 mm; length \"200/210/300/312\" mm; connection \"Flanges DN 50\"; Q3 40; Q4 50; Q3/Q1 63; totalizer range 999999 or 9999999 m³; verification interval 0.2 or 2 dm³; cyclic volume 20 dm³; sensitivity U0SD0; pressure loss <16 bar; orientation H/V; T30; env B/O.\n\nWE65: DN65; length 200/220/225/300; DN65 flanges; Q3 63; Q4 78.7; ratio 63; range 999999/9999999; v.i. 0.2/2; cyclic 20; U3SD0; <40.\n\nWE80: DN80; length 200/220/225/350; DN80 flanges; Q3 63; Q4 78.7; ratio 63; range 999999/9999999; v.i. 0.2/2; cyclic 20; U3SD0; <40.\n\nWE100: DN100; length 250/290 (shared with WE125); flanges DN100; Q3 100; Q4 125; ratio 100; range 999999/9999999; v.i. 0.2/2; cyclic 20; U0D0; <10 (DN100).\n\nWE125: DN125; length 250/290; flanges DN125; Q3 160; Q4 200; ratio 160; range 999999/9999999; v.i. 0.2/2; cyclic 20; U0D0; <16 (DN125).\n\nWE150 (Q3=250): DN150; length 300/340; flanges DN150; Q3 250; Q4 312.5; ratio 100; range 9999999*; v.i. 2*; cyclic 200; U0D0; <25; orientation H/V; T30; B/O.\n\nWE150 (Q3=400): DN150; length 300/340; flanges DN150; Q3 400; Q4 500; ratio 160; range 9999999*; v.i. 2*; cyclic 200; U0D0; <40; ...\n\nWE200: DN200; length 350; DN200 flanges; Q3 630; Q4 787.5; ratio 40; range 9999999 or 99999999; v.i. 2 or 20; cyclic 200; U0D0; Δp25; H only; water temp 0.1-30.\n\nWE250: DN250; length 450; Q3 1000; Q4 1250; ratio 40; range 9999999/99999999; v.i. 2/20; cyclic 200; U0D0; Δp25; H; 0.1-30.\n\nWE300: DN300; length 500; Q3 1600; Q4 2000; ratio 80; range 9999999/99999999; v.i. 2/20; cyclic 200; U0D0; Δp25; H; 0.1-30.\n\nFootnote markers: WE150 totalizer range \"9 999 999*\" has marker *; v.i. \"2*\" marker *. The footnote says the 99 999 999 m³ version (v.i. 20 dm³) usable up to Q1=3.125 m³/h inclusive. Hmm — the footnote marker * is on the WE150 columns. The footnote text mentions 99 999 999 — interesting: table says 9 999 999* but footnote says 99 999 999 m³ version with 20 dm³ interval... These numbers: 9 999 999 (9.99M) vs 99 999 999 (99.99M). The footnote text is about a 99 999 999 m³-range totalizer. Slight inconsistency in the doc (or the * footnote applies to both pages: page 3 has \"9 999 999 ou 99 999 999\"). The footnote appears on page 1 (WE50-150 table). So for WE150: the totalizer with 99 999 999 m³ range (v.i. 20 dm³) usable up to Q1=3.125 m³/h. But the table cell says \"9 999 999*\" — contradictory. Whatever, faithful extraction: footnote as written.\n\nFootnotes:\n- \"*\": \"La version de totalisateur avec la portée de 99 999 999 m³ (échelon de vérification 20 dm³) est utilisable jusqu'à Q₁= 3,125m³/h inclus.\" → I'll include English-ish? Rule 10: extract English version, ignore parallel translation. There's no English here (doc is French). I'll keep French text as-is? Or translate? The rule says extract English when bilingual. This doc is French-primary with some English headers but footnote only in French. I'll keep the French text.\n\nHmm, should I translate footnotes? Rules say extract the English version when bilingual; here only French exists, so keep French.\n\n- \"**\": \"Le compteur n'est pas conçu pour mesurer des flux inversés mais résiste à un flux inversé accidentel sans subir aucune détérioration ou modification des propriétés métrologiques.\"\n\nAlso the notes about Q3/Q1 lower values permitted: page 1 note (min 40) and page 3 note (min 10). These could be footnotes without markers, or captured in characteristics as \"minimum permitted q3_q1_ratio\". I'll add them as footnotes with markers? They have no markers. I could add to matrix_tables footnotes or as description. I'll add them as footnotes with pseudo-markers? Rule 7 says extract footnote definitions. These are notes, not numbered footnotes. I'll include them in the relevant matrix_tables \"footnotes\" or as certificate-level footnotes with markers \"*\" — no, they're distinct. I'll add them into matrix_tables[].footnotes for the page-1 and page-3 tables, and maybe also as footnotes[] entries with markers like \"note_page1\"? Better to keep footnotes[] for real markers (*, **) and put the ratio notes in matrix_tables footnotes. Hmm, or add a characteristics type_level note? I'll put them in matrix_tables[].description/footnotes.\n\nCertificate-level fields:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R 49/2013-FR2-15.01 rev2\" — the doc: \"N° R 49/2013-FR2-15.01 rev2\". Keep as \"R 49/2013-FR2-15.01 rev2\".\n- scheme: \"B\" (OIML-B vertical text indicates OIML-CS Scheme B).\n- project_number: null? Not present. Omit.\n- page_total: 4\n- member_state: \"FRANCE\"\n- date_issued: \"2016-10-18\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not given explicitly. LNE... omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Laboratoire National de Métrologie et d'Essais\" (LNE)\n- address_lines: [\"1, rue Gaston Boissier\", \"75724 Paris Cedex 15\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Thomas LOMMATZSCH\"\n- person_title: \"Responsable du Pôle Certification\" / \"Measuring Instruments Division Manager\" — English: \"Measuring Instruments Division Manager\". Rule: extract English. I'll use \"Measuring Instruments Division Manager\".\n- phone: \"01 40 43 37 00\" → \"+33 1 40 43 37 00\"? Keep as-is \"01 40 43 37 00\".\n- email: \"info@lne.fr\"\n- website: \"www.lne.fr\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ITRON FRANCE\", address_lines: [\"11 boulevard Pasteur\", \"67500 HAGUENAU\", \"FRANCE\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (compteur d'eau). The schema example lists \"Gas meter\", \"Measuring system\" — category \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"WOLTEX (WE)\", \"WE50\", \"MWE50\", ...]? The certified type is \"compteur d'eau ITRON type WOLTEX (WE)\". The designations: WOLTEX WE and MWE series. I'll put [\"WOLTEX (WE)\", \"WOLTEX (MWE)\"]? Hmm — MWE appears only in tables. Type designation: \"WOLTEX (WE)\". Models: WE50-WE300, MWE50-MWE300. type_designations: [\"WOLTEX (WE)\"] plus maybe \"MWE\". I'll list [\"WOLTEX (WE)\", \"WOLTEX (MWE)\"]? Hmm, MWE variant meaning unknown; tables pair WE/MWE. I'll include both.\n- module_designation: null / \"Not applicable\"? Water meter is the whole instrument under R49; no modules. The schema says \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll use null or \"Not applicable\". I'll omit? Include \"Not applicable\"? I'll set null... Actually for non-module certs, null is fine. I'll omit the field? Rule 8: if not present, omit. Omit.\n- description: \"Water meter ITRON type WOLTEX (WE)...\" — \"Characteristics: see annex\". I'll write: \"Water meter ITRON type WOLTEX (WE) and MWE, DN50 to DN300, Q3 40 to 1600 m³/h, accuracy class 2.\"  Hmm, don't fabricate — keep simple: \"Water meter ITRON type WOLTEX (WE); characteristics: see annex.\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"WOLTEX\" (ITRON type WOLTEX). \n- models[]: 10 models as above with attributes.\n\ncomponents: none. Omit.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2, unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- q2_q1_ratio: {value: 1.6}\n- max_admissible_pressure: {value: 20, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- climatic_environment_range: {value: {min:5, max:55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — applies to all models (both tables say +5…+55). ✓ type_level.\n- totalizer_type: {value: \"TVM / metal glass\"}? Hmm \"TVM / Verre Métal\" — keep raw \"TVM / Verre Métal\". Type-level? Both tables say TVM/Verre Métal ✓.\n- mechanical_environment_class: {value: \"N/A\"}\n- magnetic_environment_class: {value: \"N/A\"}\n- reverse_flow_measurement: {value: false? or \"No\"} with footnote \"**\". I'll use value \"No\" string, footnote_markers [\"**\"].\n- nominal diameter range? Could do dn: {min:50, max:300} mm as type_level, plus per-model values. I'll put per-model in model_level only.\n- reverse flow resistance — covered.\n\nWait, is mechanical/magnetic environment class and reverse flow in both tables? Page 2: yes (WE50-150). Page 4: yes (WE200-300). Same values ✓ type_level.\n\nWater temperature: differs (T30 class vs explicit range) → model_level or handle: WE50-150: water_temperature_class \"T30\"; WE200-300: water_temperature_range {min: 0.1, max: 30} °C. Note 0.1-30°C also = T30. I'll record both attributes in model_level.\n\nOrientation: model_level: WE50-150: \"Horizontal or Vertical\"; WE200-300: \"Horizontal\".\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: model_level: WE50: U0SD0; WE65: U3SD0; WE80: U3SD0; WE100: U0D0; WE125: U0D0; WE150(250): U0D0; WE150(400): U0D0; WE200: U0D0; WE250: U0D0; WE300: U0D0. Note page 4 says \"selon EN14154, S= stab. type SD3, U0D0\". Fine.\n\nPressure loss: model_level as decided; WE200-300: pressure_loss_class \"Δp25\" (string) — separate attribute pressure_loss_class with values \"Δp25\" for the big three. Hmm, or fold: pressure_loss_at_q3 for small models; pressure_loss_class for large. OK.\n\nmodel_level matrix entries:\n1. nominal_diameter (dn): unit mm: WE50:50, WE65:65, WE80:80, WE100:100, WE125:125, WE150a:150, WE150b:150, WE200:200, WE250:250, WE300:300.\n2. length: unit mm: strings: WE50 \"200/210/300/312\", WE65 \"200/220/225/300\", WE80 \"200/220/225/350\", WE100 \"250/290\", WE125 \"250/290\", WE150a \"300/340\", WE150b \"300/340\", WE200 350, WE250 450, WE300 500. (Numbers for the big three.)\n3. connection: \"Flanges DN 50\" etc. — maybe skip as characteristic; include in models' attributes. I'll put in models attributes.\n4. q3 permanent flow rate: unit m³/h: 40,63,63,100,160,250,400,630,1000,1600.\n5. q4 overload flow rate: 50, 78.7, 78.7, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000.\n6. q3_q1_ratio: 63,63,63,100,160,100,160,40,40,80.\n7. totalizer_range: unit m³: WE50-125: \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\" → \"999999 or 9999999\" string; WE150a/b: \"9 999 999\" with marker *; WE200-300: \"9 999 999 or 99 999 999\" string. Hmm — value as list? Not allowed except model matrix. Keep strings.\n\nWait, for WE50-WE125 the range cell is \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\" (colspan 5) — so those five logical types each offer both. WE150: \"9 999 999*\". WE200-300: \"9 999 999 ou 99 999 999\".\n\n8. verification_interval: unit dm³: WE50-125: \"0.2 or 2\"; WE150a/b: \"2\" with marker *; WE200-300: \"2 or 20\".\n9. cyclic_volume: unit dm³: WE50-125: 20; WE150a/b: 200; WE200-300: 200.\n10. orientation: strings.\n11. water_temperature_class: WE50-150a/b: \"T30\".\n12. water_temperature_range: WE200-300: {min: 0.1, max: 30} °C.\n13. environmental_class: WE50-150a/b: \"B/O\".\n14. flow_profile_sensitivity_class: as above.\n15. pressure_loss_at_q3: WE50:16, WE65:40, WE80:40, WE100:10, WE125:16, WE150a:25, WE150b:40 (unit bar) — with note.\n16. pressure_loss_class: WE200: \"Δp25\", WE250: \"Δp25\", WE300: \"Δp25\".\n\nHmm, the Δ character — fine in JSON.\n\nmatrix_tables: I'll include 2 tables:\n1. \"Characteristics WE50–WE150 (annex pages 1–2)\" — hmm, better: two tables as in doc: \n   a. name: \"Model characteristics (WE50–WE150)\" columns: type, dn, length, connection, totalizer, totalizer_range, verification_interval, cyclic_volume, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, q2_q1_ratio. Rows: 7 rows (including both WE150 variants? or keep the merged \"WE100-WE125\" style?). For the matrix table, preserve raw doc structure: columns as OCR'd with merged groups. I think for matrix_tables I should preserve the ORIGINAL table structure (raw), while model_level holds the clean per-model extraction. So rows for page-1 table: \n   {\"type\": \"WE50/MWE50\", \"dn_mm\": \"50\", \"length_mm\": \"200/210/300/312\", \"connections\": \"Flanges DN 50\", \"totalizers\": \"TVM / Metal Glass\", \"totalizer_range_m3\": \"999 999 or 9 999 999\", \"verification_interval_dm3\": \"0.2 or 2\", \"theoretical_cyclic_volume_dm3\": \"20\", \"q3_m3h\": \"40\", \"q4_m3h\": \"50\", \"q3_q1\": \"63\", \"q2_q1\": \"1.6\"}\n   \n   Hmm, but the OCR rows are per-attribute (transposed). matrix_tables rows = object per row; the doc's rows are attributes. I could transpose to per-model rows (cleaner). But then raw preservation is lost for merged cells... The schema: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Since most of it fits in model_level, maybe matrix_tables only needed for the ambiguous pressure-loss row? \n\n   Simpler plan: matrix_tables with per-model rows for each page-table (transposed to model-rows), using raw cell text where ambiguous. Let me define:\n\n   Table 1: \"Annex page 1 – WE50–WE150 main characteristics\" columns: [type, dn, length, connections, totalizer_type, totalizer_range, verification_interval, cyclic_volume, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, q2_q1_ratio]; 7 rows: WE50/MWE50, WE65/MWE65, WE80/MWE80, WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125 (merged as in doc? no—split into WE100 and WE125 with values from the 7-column Q3 row...). Hmm, the merged cells make per-model rows require splitting anyway (DN \"100/125\", length \"250/290\", etc.).\n\n   I'll do: rows for: \"WE50/MWE50\", \"WE65/MWE65\", \"WE80/MWE80\", \"WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125\", \"WE150/MWE150 (Q3=250)\", \"WE150/MWE150 (Q3=400)\". With raw-ish values: WE100-WE125 row: dn \"100/125\", q3 \"100 / 160\", etc. That preserves ambiguity.\n\n   Table 2: \"Annex page 2 – WE50–WE150 additional characteristics\": columns [type, orientation, accuracy_class, environmental_class, map, flow_profile_sensitivity, pressure_loss_at_q3, water_temperature_class, climatic_environment, mechanical_env_class, magnetic_env_class, reverse_flow]. Rows: WE50/MWE50, WE65/MWE65, WE80/MWE80, WE100-WE125, WE150. Values raw: e.g., WE100-125 pressure \"<0.10 (DN100) / <0.16 (DN125)\"... raw is \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\". Keep raw \"<10 (DN100) / <16 (DN125)\". WE150 row: the two pressure cells \"<25 (DN100) / <40 (DN125)\" and \"<16\" — I'll put \"…/ <16\" with description noting OCR colspan ambiguity. In description I'll note: \"Pressure-loss row contains an extra cell in the OCR; raw cells: <16 | <40 | <10 (DN100) <16 (DN125) | <25 (DN100) <40 (DN125) | <16.\"\n\n   Table 3: \"Annex page 3 – WE200–WE300 main characteristics\": columns [type, dn, length, connections, totalizer_type, totalizer_range, verification_interval, cyclic_volume, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, q2_q1_ratio]; rows WE200/MWE200, WE250/MWE250, WE300/MWE300.\n\n   Table 4: \"Annex page 4 – WE200–WE300 additional characteristics\": columns [type, orientation, accuracy_class, map, flow_profile_sensitivity, pressure_loss_class, water_temperature_range, climatic_environment, mechanical_env, magnetic_env, reverse_flow]; 3 rows.\n\nFootnotes at top level:\n- {\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"La version de totalisateur avec la portée de 99 999 999 m³ (échelon de vérification 20 dm³) est utilisable jusqu'à Q₁ = 3,125 m³/h inclus.\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"**\", \"text\": \"Le compteur n'est pas conçu pour mesurer des flux inversés mais résiste à un flux inversé accidentel sans subir aucune détérioration ou modification des propriétés métrologiques.\"}\n\nAlso ratio notes as footnotes? I'll add them to matrix_tables[].footnotes:\nTable1 footnotes: [\"Pour un débit nominal donné (Q3), des valeurs de Q3/Q1 inférieures à celles figurant dans le tableau sont permises; toutefois elles ne peuvent être inférieures à 40.\"]\nTable3 footnotes: same with 10.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical \"R49\" not \"OIML R 60\" style → \"R49\").\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null\n- scheme: \"B\"? The recommendation scheme field... The schema: recommendation: {id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]}. scheme here probably means the certificate scheme (A|B)? Given ambiguity, I'll set \"B\".\n- accuracy_classes: [2]? R49 accuracy classes — the cert says class 2. Include [2]. Hmm, accuracy_classes as ints or strings? I'll use [\"2\"]? For R60 they'd be like \"C\". Here numeric 2 → [2]. I'll use [2] ints. Hmm, could be [\"C\"]-style strings; numeric fine.\n\ntest_reports:\n- {\"id\": \"LNE n°P163010\", \"role\": \"evaluation_report\", \"pages\": 29} — \"OIML R 49 EVALUATION REPORT : LNE n°P163010 (29 pages)\". date unknown → omit date.\n- documentation files: \"Notice descriptive du type WE 50 à 300 & MWE 50 à 300_D7014908-AA_MD\" → documentation_file; \"Argumentaire Itron du 29/06/2016\" → documentation_file, date 2016-06-29; \"D7014908-AA_Approval Report Part B WE200-250-300 MID\" → documentation_file.\n\nrevision_history: certificate is rev2 but no revision details → omit.\n\ncertificate.page_total: 4. member_state: \"FRANCE\". date_issued: \"2016-10-18\". oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\nNow the WE150 naming: I'll use model_ids: \"WE150/MWE150 (Q3=250 m³/h)\" and \"WE150/MWE150 (Q3=400 m³/h)\". Hmm, but the footnote marker * on WE150 totalizer... fine.\n\nWait — actually, should I double check the WE150 two-column interpretation once more? Page 1 header: WE150 spans 2 columns; Q3 row has 7 values (40, 63, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400). WE100-WE125 spans 2 columns → WE100: 100, WE125: 160. Remaining 250, 400 for WE150's two columns. Q3/Q1 row: 63, 63, 63, 100, 160, 100, 160 → WE100: 100, WE125: 160, WE150a: 100, WE150b: 160. So WE150: Q3=250 R100 and Q3=400 R160. Q4: 312.5 and 500. ✓ consistent with Q4=1.25×Q3.\n\nHmm interesting: WE125 has Q3=160, R=160; WE150a Q3=250 R100; WE150b Q3=400 R160. Plausible for Woltex (DN150: 250 m³/h R100... hmm, or 400 m³/h R160 hmm hmm). Actually real Woltex WE DN150: Q3 = 250 m³/h or 400 m³/h. I'm fairly confident. Good.\n\nNow DN row check with 7 columns: \"50\", \"65\", \"80\", \"100/125\" (span2 → 100, 125), \"150\" (span2 → 150, 150). ✓.\n\nLength: \"200/210/300/312\" WE50; \"200/220/225/300\" WE65; \"200/220/225/350\" WE80; \"250/290\" (span2) WE100&WE125; \"300/340\" (span2) WE150a&b. ✓.\n\nConnections: \"Brides DN 50\", \"Brides DN 65\", \"Brides DN 80\", \"Brides DN 100 et DN125\" (WE100&WE125), \"Brides DN 150\" (WE150). ✓.\n\nTotalizer range: colspan5 \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\" → covers cols1-5 = WE50..WE125 ✓; colspan2 \"9 999 999*\" → WE150a&b ✓.\n\nV.I.: colspan5 \"0.2 ou 2\" → WE50-WE125; colspan2 \"2*\" → WE150. ✓.\n\nCyclic volume: colspan5 \"20\" → WE50-WE125; colspan2 \"200\" → WE150. ✓.\n\nGreat, consistent.\n\nTotalizer type row: colspan 7 \"TVM / Verre Métal\" — covers all 7. ✓.\n\nQ2/Q1: colspan7 \"1.6\" ✓.\n\nPage 2 table (5-col grid): orientation colspan5 \"Horizontale ou Verticale\"; accuracy class 2; env class \"B/O\"; MAP 20 bar; sensitivity: WE50: U0SD0; WE65+80: U3SD0; WE100-125+WE150: U0D0; pressure loss: messy; water temp class T30; climatic +5…+55; mech N/A; mag N/A; reverse flow \"Non\" with **.\n\nPage 3 (WE200-300): DN 200/250/300; length 350/450/500; flanges DN200/250/300; totalizer TVM/Verre Métal; range \"9 999 999 ou 99 999 999\"; v.i. \"2 ou 20\"; cyclic 200; Q3: 630, 1000, 1600; Q4: 787.5, 1250, 2000; Q3/Q1: 40, 40, 80; Q2/Q1: 1.6. Note: min ratio 10.\n\nPage 4: orientation Horizontale; class 2; MAP 20 bar; sensitivity U0D0 (EN14154, S=stab type SD3); pressure loss class Δp25 (EN14154 at Q3); water temp +0.1…+30 °C; climatic +5…+55; mech N/A; mag N/A; reverse flow Non**.\n\nNow build the JSON.\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"WOLTEX\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"WE50/MWE50\", \"label\": \"Woltex WE50 / MWE50\", \"attributes\": {\"dn\": 50, \"connection\": \"Flanges DN 50\"}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nKeep attributes light: dn, connection.\n\nmodels:\n1. WE50/MWE50: dn 50, connection \"Flanges DN 50\"\n2. WE65/MWE65: dn 65, \"Flanges DN 65\"\n3. WE80/MWE80: dn 80, \"Flanges DN 80\"\n4. WE100/MWE100: dn 100, \"Flanges DN 100\"\n5. WE125/MWE125: dn 125, \"Flanges DN 125\"\n6. WE150/MWE150 (Q3=250 m³/h): dn 150, \"Flanges DN 150\"\n7. WE150/MWE150 (Q3=400 m³/h): dn 150, \"Flanges DN 150\"\n8. WE200/MWE200: dn 200\n9. WE250/MWE250: dn 250\n10. WE300/MWE300: dn 300\n\ncharacteristics.model_level entries (each with values for relevant models):\n\nI'll write them now. Structured value objects: each value entry {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ...}. Wait — the schema example for model_level: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}]}. So unit at attribute level, values have {model, value, footnote_markers}. Good — unit once per attribute.\n\nmodel_level:\n\n1. nominal_diameter: unit \"mm\": WE50:50, WE65:65, WE80:80, WE100:100, WE125:125, WE150(250):150, WE150(400):150, WE200:200, WE250:250, WE300:300.\n2. length: unit \"mm\": WE50 \"200/210/300/312\", WE65 \"200/220/225/300\", WE80 \"200/220/225/350\", WE100 \"250/290\", WE125 \"250/290\", WE150(250) \"300/340\", WE150(400) \"300/340\", WE200 350, WE250 450, WE300 500.\n3. permanent_flow_rate_q3: unit \"m³/h\": values 40, 63, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600.\n4. overload_flow_rate_q4: unit \"m³/h\": 50, 78.75? — doc says 78.7 (WE65, WE80). Keep 78.7. Then 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000.\n5. q3_q1_ratio: 63, 63, 63, 100, 160, 100, 160, 40, 40, 80.\n6. totalizer_range: unit \"m³\": WE50-125: \"999999 or 9999999\" (string) ×5; WE150a: \"9999999\", marker *; WE150b: \"9999999\", marker *; WE200-300: \"9999999 or 99999999\".\n\nHmm — footnote * marker format: doc uses \"*\" (the cells show \"9 999 999*\" and \"2*\"). The footnote definition is given as \"\\* La version...\". I'll use marker \"*\".\n\nShould I normalize \"999 999\" → 999999? For strings I'll keep with spaces as in doc: \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\"? Rule 10 multilingual — translate \"ou\" → \"or\"? The doc is French; extract English version if exists — there's no English for cell values. I'll keep French \"ou\"? Hmm, that mixes. I think normalizing to \"999 999 or 9 999 999\" is reasonable (minimal translation for readability). I'll do that.\n\n7. verification_interval: unit \"dm³\": WE50-125: \"0.2 or 2\" ×5; WE150a/b: 2, marker *; WE200-300: \"2 or 20\".\n8. cyclic_volume: unit \"dm³\": 20 ×5 (WE50-125), 200 for WE150a, WE150b, WE200, WE250, WE300.\n9. orientation: WE50-150b: \"Horizontal or Vertical\" ×7; WE200-300: \"Horizontal\" ×3.\n10. environmental_class: WE50-150b: \"B/O\" ×7.\n11. water_temperature_class: WE50-150b: \"T30\" ×7.\n12. water_temperature_range: unit \"°C\": WE200: {min:0.1, max:30}, WE250: same, WE300: same.\n13. flow_profile_sensitivity_class: WE50 \"U0SD0\"; WE65 \"U3SD0\"; WE80 \"U3SD0\"; WE100 \"U0D0\"; WE125 \"U0D0\"; WE150a \"U0D0\"; WE150b \"U0D0\"; WE200 \"U0D0\"; WE250 \"U0D0\"; WE300 \"U0D0\".\n14. pressure_loss_at_q3: unit \"bar\": WE50 {max:16}, WE65 {max:40}, WE80 {max:40}, WE100 {max:10}, WE125 {max:16}, WE150a {max:25}, WE150b {max:40}. Footnote? I'll add footnote_markers? No footnote in doc for these. Hmm, given the OCR ambiguity, maybe include marker? No — don't fabricate. The matrix_tables will carry the raw.\n\nHmm wait, actually should I really assign 25/40 to the two WE150 variants? The raw order was [<25 (DN100)<40 (DN125)][<16]. If I map strictly: the cell \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" — if this cell were on the 5-col grid at position 4 (WE100-WE125)... then position 3 would be... cells: 1:<16(WE50), 2-3:<40(WE65,80), 4:<10DN100/<16DN125(WE100-125), 5:<25DN100/<40DN125(WE150???), 6:<16(extra). The 6th cell has no column → maybe it actually belongs to... nothing, or the 5th cell \"…(DN100)/(DN125)\" belongs to WE150 incorrectly.\n\nUnder the 7-col grid: 1:<16, 2-3:<40, 4:<10DN100, 5:<16DN125, 6:<25??, 7:<40??... but cells 4 and 5 are one td: \"<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)\". Hmm so 4-5 as one td covering WE100-WE125 (which spans 2 cols ✓!). Then td \"<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)\" would cover col6 only or 6-7? and final td \"<16\" the last one.\n\nIf \"<25<40\" td covers col6-7 (WE150 span 2 ✓): WE150a: <25, WE150b: <40 — consistent with span! Then trailing \"<16\" is orphan/extra — possibly OCR duplication. The annotations \"(DN100)/(DN125)\" inside the WE150 cell are then OCR errors (should probably be different), OR the annotations belong to the WE100-125 cell and got duplicated.\n\nGiven the td spans, mapping WE150a:<25, WE150b:<40 is structurally supported (colspan2 for WE150 in header page 1; but page 2 header has WE150 as single column, grid 5...). Ugh, conflicting grids: page-2 sensitivity row uses 5-grid (1+2+2), pressure row uses 6-grid (1+2+1+1+1) — inconsistent within the same table! One of them is broken OCR.\n\nWhichever way, both interpretations give WE150 pressure values from {16, 25, 40}. I'll go with 25 and 40 for the two variants (matches increasing loss with Q3, matches physical Δp classes), noting raw in matrix_tables. Actually hmm, alternatively WE150 single value <16?? If the trailing <16 aligns under WE150 in a 5-col grid: [<16][<40 span2][<10DN100 <16DN125][<25DN100 <40DN125][<16] → positions: WE50:<16; WE65-80:<40; WE100-125:<10/<16; WE150???: we have TWO cells for one column — nope, doesn't fit; the 6-grid fits better with orphan.\n\nGoing with 25/40. And I'll set matrix_tables description to note raw row content.\n\n15. pressure_loss_class: WE200/250/300: \"Δp25\".\n16. maximum_admissible_pressure — type level (20 bar all). ✓ type_level.\n\ntype_level:\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- totalizer_type: \"TVM / Verre Métal\" — hmm, translate \"Verre Métal\" = \"metal glass\"? Keep raw \"TVM / Verre Métal\". I'll keep raw.\n- max_admissible_pressure: 20 bar\n- climatic_environment: {min:5, max:55} °C — label \"climatic_environment_range\"? Page 2 \"Environnement climatique: +5°C … +55°C\". Label: \"climatic_environment_temperature_range\". Hmm keep \"climatic_environment_range\". I'll use \"climatic_operating_temperature_range\". Simpler: \"climatic_environment_range\".\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"N/A\"\n- magnetic_environment_class: \"N/A\"\n- reverse_flow_measurement: \"No\", footnote_markers [\"**\"]\n\nAlso maybe \"minimum_permitted_q3_q1_ratio\": 40 for WE50-150 and 10 for WE200-300 (from notes) → model_level? These come from notes; I'll capture in matrix_tables footnotes and skip as characteristics... Actually could add as model_level attribute \"minimum_permitted_q3_q1_ratio\": 40 for the 7 small, 10 for the 3 large. That's informative and derived directly from the notes. I'll include it.\n\nNow matrix_tables. Let me write them compactly but completely.\n\nTable A: name \"Main characteristics — WE50 to WE150 (annex page 1)\"\ncolumns: [\"type\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"length\", \"connections\", \"totalizer_type\", \"totalizer_range\", \"verification_interval\", \"cyclic_volume\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q2_q1_ratio\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", null, null, \"m³\", \"dm³\", \"dm³\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null]\nrows:\n- {\"type\": \"WE50/MWE50\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"50\", \"length\": \"200/210/300/312\", \"connections\": \"Brides DN 50\", \"totalizer_type\": \"TVM / Verre Métal\", \"totalizer_range\": \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\", \"verification_interval\": \"0.2 ou 2\", \"cyclic_volume\": \"20\", \"q3\": \"40\", \"q4\": \"50\", \"q3_q1_ratio\": \"63\", \"q2_q1_ratio\": \"1.6\"}\n\nHmm, raw strings with French \"ou\"... For matrix tables, raw preservation with French is OK (description says preserve tables). I'll keep raw exactly: \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\". Yes, raw.\n\n- WE65/MWE50... careful: WE65/MWE65: dn 65, length \"200/220/225/300\", \"Brides DN 65\", ..., q3 63, q4 78.7, ratio 63.\n- WE80/MWE80: 80, \"200/220/225/350\", \"Brides DN 80\", 63, 78.7, 63.\n- WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125: dn \"100/125\", length \"250/290\", \"Brides DN 100 et DN 125\", range \"999 999 ou 9 999 999\", vi \"0.2 ou 2\", cv 20, q3 \"100 / 160\", q4 \"125 / 200\", ratio \"100 / 160\", q2q1 1.6.\n- WE150/MWE150 (Q3=250 m³/h): dn 150, length \"300/340\", \"Brides DN 150\", range \"9 999 999*\", vi \"2*\", cv 200, q3 250, q4 312.5, ratio 100.\n- WE150/MWE150 (Q3=400 m³/h): 150, \"300/340\", \"Brides DN 150\", \"9 999 999*\", \"2*\", 200, 400, 500, 160.\n\nfootnotes: [\"Pour un débit nominal donné (Q3), des valeurs de Q3/Q1 inférieures à celles figurant dans le tableau ci-dessus sont permises. Toutefois les valeurs de ce ratio ne peuvent être inférieures à 40.\", \"* La version de totalisateur avec la portée de 99 999 999 m³ (échelon de vérification 20 dm³) est utilisable jusqu'à Q1 = 3,125 m³/h inclus.\"]\n\ndescription: \"Values for WE100 and WE125 are merged in the source table ('100/125'); Q3/Q4/ratio columns each carry two values (WE100 first, WE125 second). WE150 exists in two flow-rate variants (Q3 = 250 and 400 m³/h).\"\n\nTable B: name \"Additional characteristics — WE50 to WE150 (annex page 2)\"\ncolumns: [\"type\", \"orientation\", \"accuracy_class\", \"environmental_class\", \"max_admissible_pressure\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"pressure_loss_at_q3\", \"water_temperature_class\", \"climatic_environment\", \"mechanical_environment_class\", \"magnetic_environment_class\", \"reverse_flow_measurement\"]\nrows:\n- WE50/MWE50: H ou V... raw \"Horizontale ou Verticale\", \"2\", \"B/O\", \"20\", \"U0SD0\", \"<16\", \"T30\", \"+5°C … +55°C\", \"N/A\", \"N/A\", \"Non**\"\n- WE65/MWE65: \"U3SD0\", \"<40\"\n- WE80/MWE80: \"U3SD0\", \"<40\"\n- WE100-WE125/MWE100-MWE125: \"U0D0\", \"<10 (DN100) / <16 (DN125)\"\n- WE150/MWE150: \"U0D0\", \"<25 (DN100) / <40 (DN125) / <16\" — description notes the OCR carried an extra cell.\n\ndescription: \"Pressure-loss row as OCR'd contains cells: '<16' | '<40' (span 2) | '<10 (DN100) <16 (DN125)' | '<25 (DN100) <40 (DN125)' | '<16'. Values likely correspond to EN 14154 pressure-loss classes (Δp16, Δp40, Δp10, Δp16, Δp25, Δp40) with the decimal point lost in OCR.\"\n\nfootnotes: [\"** Le compteur n'est pas conçu pour mesurer des flux inversés mais résiste à un flux inversé accidentel sans subir aucune détérioration ou modification des propriétés métrologiques.\"]\n\nTable C: \"Main characteristics — WE200 to WE300 (annex page 3)\"\ncolumns: [\"type\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"length\", \"connections\", \"totalizer_type\", \"totalizer_range\", \"verification_interval\", \"cyclic_volume\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q2_q1_ratio\"]\nrows:\n- WE200/MWE200: 200, 350, \"Brides DN 200\", \"TVM / Verre Métal\", \"9 999 999 ou 99 999 999\", \"2 ou 20\", 200, 630, 787.5, 40, 1.6\n- WE250/MWE250: 250, 450, \"Brides DN 250\", ..., 1000, 1250, 40, 1.6\n- WE300/MWE300: 300, 500, \"Brides DN 300\", ..., 1600, 2000, 80, 1.6\nfootnotes: [ratio note with 10]\n\nTable D: \"Additional characteristics — WE200 to WE300 (annex page 4)\"\ncolumns: [\"type\", \"orientation\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max_admissible_pressure\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"water_temperature_range\", \"climatic_environment\", \"mechanical_environment_class\", \"magnetic_environment_class\", \"reverse_flow_measurement\"]\nrows:\n- WE200/MWE200: \"Horizontale\", \"2\", \"20\", \"U0D0 (selon EN 14154, S = stab. type SD3)\", \"Δp25\", \"+0.1°C … +30°C\", \"+5°C …+55°C\", \"N/A\", \"N/A\", \"Non**\"\n- WE250/MWE250: same\n- WE300/MWE300: same\nfootnotes: [\"** Le compteur n'est pas conçu...\"]\n\ntest_reports:\n[\n {\"id\": \"LNE n°P163010\", \"role\": \"evaluation_report\", \"pages\": 29},\n {\"id\": \"Notice descriptive du type WE 50 à 300 & MWE 50 à 300 — D7014908-AA_MD\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"},\n {\"id\": \"Argumentaire Itron du 29/06/2016\", \"date\": \"2016-06-29\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"},\n {\"id\": \"D7014908-AA_Approval Report Part B WE200-250-300 MID\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"B\", \"accuracy_classes\": [2]}\n\ncertificate: number \"R 49/2013-FR2-15.01 rev2\", scheme \"B\", page_total 4, member_state \"FRANCE\", date_issued \"2016-10-18\".\n\nAlso maybe include \"accreditation\": cofrac n°5-0012 — not in schema; skip. Could add to issuing_authority? Not in schema fields; skip (rule 8).\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Water meter ITRON type WOLTEX (WE); characteristics: see annex.\" Also note MWE. I'll write: \"Water meter ITRON type WOLTEX (WE); characteristics: see annex.\"\n\ntype_designations: [\"WOLTEX (WE)\", \"WE50\", \"WE65\", \"WE80\", \"WE100\", \"WE125\", \"WE150\", \"WE200\", \"WE250\", \"WE300\", \"MWE50\", ..., \"MWE300\"]. Maybe keep it to [\"WOLTEX (WE)\",